      "id": 1,
      "text": "Stephens County was named in honor of Alexander Hamilton Stephens, a Georgia statesman, making it Georgia's 143rd county.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        10,
        13
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1",
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://stephenscountyga.gov/history/",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The town of Toccoa, the county seat, began as a railroad stop called Dry Pond before growing into a city.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        12
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://stephenscountyga.gov/history/",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Avalon, a historic community in the county, was founded in 1882 by Richard Dempsey Yow and officially incorporated in 1909.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.georgiaencyclopedia.org/articles/counties-cities-neighborhoods/stephens-county/",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Stephens County’s rolling foothills offer 900‑foot‑high Currahee Mountain, a landmark that inspired the U.S. Army Rangers.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
